00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.440
(Brady: When you think about the constraints on a sudoku&nbsp;
grid, and I- obviously there's the famous one, you've)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:12.720
(got to have 1 to 9 in these rows, columns.&nbsp;
But this is another constraint on a sudoku. It's)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:17.280
(amazing that so many are possible, it's amazing&nbsp;
they can make so many different ones.)
- Oh yeah&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:23.040
the the world of sudoku um it's almost a sort of&nbsp;
bottomless pit. We've just done the Phistomafel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:28.000
ring and we saw how sets of different digits can&nbsp;
be important. I'm going to show you something mad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:33.320
now which is where sudoku constructors have taken&nbsp;
this sort of thinking. I don't know if you can see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:38.040
this this grid here, but there's a few things&nbsp;
to know about the the rules of sudoku as they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:43.160
apply in this grid. So you can see there are&nbsp;
some cages in the grid; so the digits in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:51.008
cage have to sum to Y to the power of 2, the&nbsp;
digits in this cage have to sum to X to the power 2.

00:00:54.312 --> 00:00:58.760
And the only other thing you really need to&nbsp;
know is that these- see these arrows with circles?&nbsp;

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:04.120
The digits along the arrow have to sum up to&nbsp;
the digit in the circle. So if this was a 1 and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:10.040
this was a 2, this would be equal to 3. This&nbsp;
puzzle involved trying to work out what X and Y&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:17.160
were; and using the Phistomafel ring and the ideas&nbsp;
of set theory you can work it out. And that's what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:23.000
I'll try and show you now. 
- (It also has to obey&nbsp;
the normal sudoku rules of the columns, the rows)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:28.960
(and the squares?)
- Yes it does, it does indeed. Let me&nbsp;
start: so this time we're going to highlight the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:36.440
first four rows of the grid using the red pen. And&nbsp;
again we know that the the red cells are four sets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:42.840
of the digits 1 to 9 altogether. And now our green&nbsp;
cells this time are not going to be quite the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:50.280
same. So we- I'm going to highlight five complete&nbsp;
columns. So in this instance we've got four sets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:56.800
of the digits 1 to 9 in the red cells, and five sets&nbsp;
of the digits 1 to 9 in the green columns. And we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:06.280
can see if we look carefully again - let's use my&nbsp;
blue pen - this portion of the grid has two colours&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:13.920
in every cell. So we can remove all of these from&nbsp;
both sets and we know that the difference between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:20.640
the red set of tiles and the green set of tiles&nbsp;
is still one set of the digits 1 to 9; because we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:26.680
had four sets versus five sets, we've removed the&nbsp;
same thing from both, so the difference is still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:33.840
one set of the digits 1 to 9. I want to imagine&nbsp;
the we remove this box 1, this box here, from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:39.760
the red set. So now what's the difference between&nbsp;
the red tiles we've got remaining and the green&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:45.200
tiles we've got remaining is now two sets of the&nbsp;
digits 1 to 9. So we're going into mad mad lands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:51.920
now. But we can actually use this to solve this&nbsp;
puzzle; because what we've got remaining in red is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:58.640
just this cage which we know sums to Y to the power of&nbsp;2. And what we've got left in the bottom of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:05.640
grid is all all of those squares. So it's all of&nbsp;
these squares versus this cage, the difference is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:10.320
two sets of the digits 1 to 9. And this is where&nbsp;
constructors get even more crazy, because they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:18.360
say okay well what would happen if we equalised&nbsp;
those differences? So what we could do is add the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:31.040
whole of column 9 into red. And what we could do is&nbsp;
add the whole of row 9 into red. And if we did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:39.240
that - this cell is obviously twice in red now this&nbsp;
cell here - but if we do that red and green are now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:45.880
equivalent again, because we've added one complete&nbsp;
set- one more complete set of red into into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:51.040
red set and another complete row of the sudoku&nbsp;
that's another complete set of the digits 1 to 9&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:57.600
into the red set. So at the moment the red and the&nbsp;
green are now equivalent. And now you can play some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:07.240
games to isolate this cage, which I- hopefully you&nbsp;
can see is X to the power of 2. And the way that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:16.000
do that is you take out, just as we did before, you&nbsp;
take out the cells from both that are common&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:29.000
which is this one, this one, this one, this one, this&nbsp;
one, this one, this one, and this one. And if you look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:35.520
very carefully now you can see that these arrows&nbsp;
which have to add up to the same number, these two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:42.960
digits here sum up to the same as a digit in the&nbsp;
green set. So if we were to take these two digits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:50.880
out of red and take this digit out of green, the&nbsp;
sets still should sum to the same number. We can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:58.200
take these two out and knock this one out. And&nbsp;
look at the what we're left in the corner, we've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:06.200
got this cell here which was twice in red and&nbsp;
once in green; so if we cancel that once from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:13.840
red and once from green we end up with it being&nbsp;
once in red. And it cancels out with its arrow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:24.000
here like that, and we now have an equation where&nbsp;
we have proved that what is left in red is exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:31.760
equal to what is left in green. And this is the&nbsp;
difference of squares, because you can see that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:44.680
x^2 is equal to y^2 + x + y from these cages.&nbsp;
And that allows you if- you you can solve that

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:52.840
equation and you'll find out that X and Y differ&nbsp;
by exactly 1 and that allows you to solve the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.840 --> 00:06:02.640
puzzle. 
- (Simple)
- Not simple, but actually&nbsp;
once you start to understand Phistomafel&nbsp;-

